 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required; the system targets delivery within 15 seconds under normal connectivity.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The delivery approach shapes the other three components. The change store maintains replayable revision sequences and a publication outbox so that a WebSocket server can fan out each committed edit and replay missed revisions on reconnect. The delivery layer manages WebSocket sessions and handles fan-out from the store's outbox. The browser layer opens a WebSocket, catches up on reconnect by replaying missed revisions, and surfaces connection state so a disconnected board is never mistaken for an unchanged one.
 
