 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed edits are pushed to connected browsers over a WebSocket connection managed on the project's own infrastructure. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds several seconds of lag and imposes repeated read load; a hosted realtime service (e.g., Ably, Pusher) was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider coupling. The accepted trade-off is owning connection-capacity and reconnect operations, which requires the change store to maintain replayable revision sequences and the browser layer to implement reconnect/catch-up logic.
